</div>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-Overview">Overview</h1>
<p>The AI+ project employs a fork-based development model that has proven s=
uccessful across numerous large-scale open source projects. This approach p=
rioritizes code quality, maintainability, and scalability while providing c=
lear audit trails and predictable release cycles.</p>
<p>Our branching strategy centers around keeping the upstream repository cl=
ean and purpose-built. Rather than cluttering the main repository with deve=
lopment branches, feature branches, and experimental work, we maintain only=
 the essential branches needed for production releases. All development wor=
k occurs on personal forks, which developers maintain independently. This s=
eparation ensures that the upstream repository serves as a reliable source =
of truth for production-ready code.</p>
<p>The model emphasizes peer review as a fundamental quality gate. Every li=
ne of code that enters the upstream repository must pass through the pull r=
equest process, where it receives scrutiny from other team members. This re=
quirement applies universally, including emergency situations where the nat=
ural inclination might be to bypass review processes. Experience has shown =
that even in crisis situations, the few extra minutes required for code rev=
iew prevent far more problems than they cause.</p>
<p>During active release processes, we implement a complete freeze on mergi=
ng to the main branch. This freeze serves multiple purposes: it provides st=
ability during the critical release preparation period, ensures that releas=
e branches contain exactly the intended code, and eliminates the confusion =
that can arise from parallel development activities. While this might seem =
restrictive, the practice has proven essential for maintaining release qual=
ity and predictability.</p>
<p>The strategy acknowledges that bugs often affect multiple releases simul=
taneously. Rather than attempting to manage a single fix across multiple br=
anches, we create separate JIRA tickets for each affected release. This app=
roach provides clear tracking, enables different fix approaches for differe=
nt releases when necessary, and ensures that testing and deployment activit=
ies are properly coordinated across all affected versions.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-RepositoryStructure">Repository =
Structure</h1>
<p>The upstream repository maintains a minimal branch structure that reflec=
ts only the essential long-term branches needed for production support. The=
 main branch serves as the primary development trunk, always containing the=
 latest stable development code. This branch represents the next release ca=
ndidate and should remain in a deployable state at all times.</p>
<p>Release branches are created when the team decides to prepare a specific=
 version for production deployment. These branches follow the naming conven=
tion <code>release/&lt;version&gt;</code> and include examples such as <cod=
e>release/1.0</code>, <code>release/2.0</code>, and <code>release/2.1</code=
>. Each release branch represents a supported production version and remain=
s active for the entire lifecycle of that release, receiving security updat=
es, critical bug fixes, and other maintenance activities as needed.</p>
<p>Personal forks contain the full spectrum of development work. Each devel=
oper maintains their own fork of every repository they work with, and these=
 forks serve as the staging area for all development activities. Topic bran=
ches within these forks follow descriptive naming patterns that include the=
 JIRA ticket identifier and a brief description of the work. Feature branch=
es might be named <code>feature/AI-1234-user-authentication</code>, while b=
ug fixes use the pattern <code>bugfix/AI-5678-memory-leak-fix</code>. For c=
ritical issues requiring immediate attention, hotfix branches follow the pa=
ttern <code>hotfix/AI-9999-security-patch</code>.</p>
<p>The fork structure provides developers with complete freedom to experime=
nt, create intermediate commits, and maintain whatever branch organization =
works best for their individual workflow. Since this work occurs entirely w=
ithin personal forks, it does not impact other team members or pollute the =
upstream repository with development artifacts.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-VersioningStrategy">Versioning S=
trategy</h1>
<p>AI+ employs a comprehensive versioning system that tracks both developme=
nt activities and release milestones through a unified format. The primary =
version format follows <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH-BUILD</code> where each comp=
onent serves a specific purpose in tracking the evolution of the codebase a=
nd coordinating release activities.</p>
<p>The MAJOR version increments for significant changes or breaking updates=
 that fundamentally alter the system's behavior or require substantial cust=
omer migration efforts. These increments typically coincide with major arch=
itectural changes, API breaking modifications, or significant feature overh=
auls that impact backward compatibility. Major version changes signal to us=
ers that they should expect substantial differences and may need to update =
their integration approaches.</p>
<p>The MINOR version increments for new features that maintain backward com=
patibility with existing functionality. These changes expand the system's c=
apabilities without disrupting existing integrations or requiring customer =
code modifications. Minor version increments represent the planned feature =
releases that deliver new value while preserving the stability that existin=
g users depend on.</p>
<p>The PATCH version increments for bug fixes, security updates, and small =
improvements that do not introduce new functionality. These changes focus o=
n maintaining and improving the reliability, performance, and security of e=
xisting features. Patch releases provide critical updates that users can ad=
opt with confidence that their existing integrations will continue to funct=
ion unchanged.</p>
<p>The BUILD component is automatically incremented by TeamCity on each bui=
ld and provides fine-grained tracking of development activities between for=
mal releases. This automatic incrementation ensures that every build receiv=
es a unique identifier, enabling precise tracking of which specific build c=
ontains particular changes or fixes. The build number serves the developmen=
t team's need to identify specific versions during testing, debugging, and =
deployment activities.</p>
<p>Only the BUILD segment receives automatic modification by the build syst=
em. The MAJOR, MINOR, and PATCH components require manual updates by the de=
velopment team based on the nature and scope of the changes being released.=
 This manual control ensures that version increments reflect intentional de=
cisions about the significance and impact of each release rather than arbit=
rary automated changes.</p>
<p>During development phases, versions include a <code>-SNAPSHOT</code> suf=
fix to indicate work in progress that has not yet been formally released. T=
he snapshot designation helps differentiate between stable releases and dev=
elopment builds, preventing confusion about which versions are appropriate =
for production use. When a version is formally released, the snapshot suffi=
x is removed to indicate that the version represents a stable, tested relea=
se.</p>
<p>The versioning system integrates with the branching strategy to provide =
clear identification of which code belongs to which release. Release branch=
es use the target release version in their names, such as <code>release/2.1=
.0</code>, creating a direct correlation between the branch structure and t=
he versioning scheme. This correlation simplifies navigation between differ=
ent versions and helps team members understand the relationship between bra=
nches and releases.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-BranchingModel">Branching Model<=
/h1>
<p>The main branch serves as the authoritative source for the next release =
under development. All feature development ultimately targets this branch, =
and it maintains a state where it could theoretically be released at any ti=
me. This requirement drives discipline in the development process, as teams=
 must ensure that incomplete features either remain disabled through featur=
e flags or are developed in a way that does not compromise the overall syst=
em stability.</p>
<p>Protection of the main branch occurs through repository settings that re=
quire pull request reviews before any code can be merged. This protection c=
annot be bypassed, even by repository administrators, ensuring that all cod=
e changes receive appropriate scrutiny regardless of urgency. The branch al=
so requires that all status checks pass before merging, providing an additi=
onal quality gate through automated testing and validation processes.</p>
<p>Release branches are created at the point when the team decides that the=
 main branch contains all the features intended for a specific release. The=
 timing of this decision typically occurs one to two weeks before the inten=
ded release date, providing time for final testing and stabilization activi=
ties. Once created, the release branch becomes the authoritative source for=
 that specific version, and all subsequent work related to that release tar=
gets the release branch rather than main.</p>
<p>The creation of a release branch triggers the freeze of the main branch =
for merging activities. This freeze ensures that the release branch contain=
s exactly the intended code and prevents the confusion that could arise fro=
m parallel development activities. During the freeze period, developers con=
tinue their work on feature branches, but these branches cannot be merged u=
ntil the release process completes and the freeze lifts.</p>
<p>Fork management requires developers to maintain personal forks that stay=
 synchronized with the upstream repository. This synchronization should occ=
ur daily and involves pulling the latest changes from upstream branches and=
 pushing those updates to the corresponding branches in the personal fork. =
Regular synchronization prevents the accumulation of large divergences that=
 can make merge conflicts more difficult to resolve.</p>
<p>Topic branch creation follows specific patterns based on the type of wor=
k being performed. Feature branches are created from the main branch and re=
present new functionality intended for the next release. Bug fix branches m=
ay be created from either the main branch or a specific release branch, dep=
ending on where the bug was discovered and which versions require the fix. =
Hotfix branches are always created from the release branch that requires th=
e emergency fix.</p>
<p>The lifecycle of a topic branch begins with creation from the appropriat=
e base branch, continues through development and testing activities, progre=
sses through the pull request and review process, and concludes with the de=
letion of the branch after successful merge. This lifecycle ensures that to=
pic branches serve their intended purpose without accumulating as long-term=
 maintenance burdens.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-DevelopmentWorkflow">Development=
 Workflow</h1>
<p>See: <a href=3D"https://perifery.atlassian.net/wiki/wiki/spaces/AI/pages=
/3642195983/Using+Git" data-linked-resource-id=3D"3642195983" data-linked-r=
esource-version=3D"2" data-linked-resource-type=3D"page">Using Git</a></p>
<p>Daily development begins with synchronization activities that ensure the=
 developer's fork reflects the current state of the upstream repository. Th=
is synchronization involves checking out the main branch, pulling the lates=
t changes from the upstream main branch, and pushing those changes to the o=
rigin main branch in the personal fork. If the developer is working with re=
lease branches, the same synchronization process applies to those branches =
as well.</p>
<p>Topic branch creation requires careful consideration of the appropriate =
base branch. Features intended for the next release should branch from main=
, while fixes for current releases should branch from the appropriate relea=
se branch. The choice of base branch determines where the eventual pull req=
uest will target and affects how the changes integrate with ongoing develop=
ment activities.</p>
<p>The development process emphasizes atomic commits that represent complet=
e, logical changes to the codebase. Each commit should be self-contained an=
d should not leave the system in a broken state. Commit messages follow a s=
tandard format that includes the JIRA ticket identifier and a clear descrip=
tion of the change. This discipline in commit creation pays dividends durin=
g debugging activities, code reviews, and historical analysis of the codeba=
se.</p>
<p>Before submitting a pull request, developers must ensure their topic bra=
nch remains current with its base branch. This requirement involves rebasin=
g the topic branch against the latest version of the base branch and resolv=
ing any conflicts that arise. The rebase process also provides an opportuni=
ty to clean up the commit history by squashing related commits or reorderin=
g commits to improve logical flow.</p>
<p>Pull request submission involves creating a detailed description that ex=
plains the purpose of the changes, the approach taken to implement them, an=
d any testing that has been performed. The description should reference the=
 relevant JIRA tickets and provide sufficient context for reviewers to unde=
rstand the changes without requiring extensive investigation. Screenshots, =
test results, and other supporting documentation enhance the quality of the=
 pull request and facilitate more effective reviews.</p>
<p>The code review process serves as a critical quality gate that ensures a=
ll changes meet the project's standards for functionality, maintainability,=
 and security. Reviewers examine not only the correctness of the implementa=
tion but also its alignment with architectural principles, its impact on pe=
rformance, and its potential for introducing technical debt. The review pro=
cess is collaborative, with reviewers providing constructive feedback and d=
evelopers responding thoughtfully to suggestions.</p>
<p>Integration of approved changes occurs through the "squash and merge" st=
rategy, which combines all commits from the topic branch into a single comm=
it on the target branch. This approach provides a clean history on the main=
 and release branches while preserving the detailed commit history within t=
he pull request for future reference. After successful integration, the top=
ic branch is deleted to prevent accumulation of stale branches.</p>
<p>Multi-repository coordination becomes necessary when features span multi=
ple repositories or when changes in one repository depend on changes in ano=
ther. This coordination requires careful planning of the development sequen=
ce, creation of matching topic branches across repositories, and synchroniz=
ed timing of pull request submission and merging. Feature flags can help ma=
nage dependencies by allowing partial functionality to be merged while rela=
ted changes are still in development.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-ReleaseProcess">Release Process<=
/h1>
<p>Release preparation begins several weeks before the intended release dat=
e with planning activities that define the scope of the release and establi=
sh the timeline for various milestones. The planning process identifies whi=
ch features will be included, which bugs must be fixed, and what testing ac=
tivities will be required. This planning culminates in the creation of a re=
lease milestone in JIRA that tracks all the work items intended for the rel=
ease.</p>
<p>Feature freeze occurs when the team determines that all intended feature=
s have been developed and merged to the main branch. The timing of the feat=
ure freeze depends on the complexity of the release and the amount of testi=
ng required, but typically occurs one to two weeks before the intended rele=
ase date. After the feature freeze, only bug fixes and stabilization work c=
an be merged to the main branch.</p>
<p>The main branch freeze represents the transition from general developmen=
t activities to focused release preparation. When the freeze begins, all pe=
nding pull requests targeting the main branch are either merged quickly or =
deferred to the next release. The freeze announcement goes out to all team =
members through multiple channels to ensure awareness and compliance.</p>
<p>Release branch creation occurs immediately after the main branch freeze =
and involves creating the <code>release/&lt;version&gt;</code> branch from =
the current head of the main branch. This creation happens simultaneously a=
cross all repositories to ensure consistency. The new release branch become=
s the focus of all release-related activities, including bug fixes, testing=
, and final preparations.</p>
<p>Version number updates occur within the release branch to reflect the tr=
ansition from development versions to release versions. This process involv=
es removing the <code>-SNAPSHOT</code> suffix from build versions and updat=
ing release version numbers to their final values. These changes are commit=
ted to the release branch and represent the first commits that differentiat=
e the release branch from main.</p>
<p>Staging deployment uses the release branch to deploy to staging environm=
ents where final testing occurs. This testing includes not only functional =
validation but also performance testing, security scanning, and integration=
 testing with external systems. Any issues discovered during this testing r=
esult in bug fixes that target the release branch.</p>
<p>Release candidate tagging provides waypoints during the stabilization pr=
ocess and enables specific builds to be deployed for testing. Release candi=
dates follow the naming pattern <code>v2.1.0-rc1</code>, <code>v2.1.0-rc2</=
code>, and so forth. Each release candidate represents a potential final re=
lease and receives thorough testing before the decision to either release i=
t or create another candidate.</p>
<p>Production release occurs when the team determines that a release candid=
ate has passed all required testing and approval processes. The final relea=
se tag removes the release candidate suffix and represents the official ver=
sion that will be deployed to production environments. The tag creation tri=
ggers deployment processes and marks the formal completion of the release.<=
/p>
<p>Post-release activities include unfreezing the main branch, updating ver=
sion numbers for the next development cycle, and cherry-picking any critica=
l fixes from the release branch back to main. The release branch remains ac=
tive to support ongoing maintenance of that release version, but the main b=
ranch returns to normal development activities.</p>
<p>Release branch maintenance continues throughout the supported lifecycle =
of each release. This maintenance includes applying security patches, fixin=
g critical bugs, and making other necessary updates. Each maintenance updat=
e results in a new patch version tag and follows the same testing and appro=
val processes as major releases.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-BugManagement">Bug Management</h=
1>
<p>The management of bugs that affect multiple releases requires a systemat=
ic approach that ensures proper tracking, coordinated development, and sync=
hronized deployment across all affected versions. When a bug is discovered =
that impacts multiple release branches and the main development branch, the=
 team creates separate JIRA tickets for each affected version rather than a=
ttempting to manage the fix through a single ticket.</p>
<p>This multi-ticket approach provides several advantages over single-ticke=
t management. Each ticket can track the specific testing requirements for i=
ts target version, the deployment timeline for that version, and any versio=
n-specific considerations that might affect the fix implementation. The app=
roach also provides clear accountability for each version and ensures that =
the fix receives appropriate attention across all affected releases.</p>
<p>JIRA ticket creation follows a consistent naming pattern that identifies=
 the affected version and links related tickets together. For a security vu=
lnerability affecting releases 1.0, 1.1, 2.0, and the main branch, the team=
 would create tickets such as "AI-5001: Fix XSS vulnerability (release/1.0)=
", "AI-5002: Fix XSS vulnerability (release/1.1)", "AI-5003: Fix XSS vulner=
ability (release/2.0)", and "AI-5004: Fix XSS vulnerability (main)". These =
tickets are linked in JIRA to show their relationship and ensure coordinate=
d management.</p>
<p>The development approach for multi-release fixes depends on the nature o=
f the bug and the differences between the affected versions. For bugs that =
can be fixed identically across all versions, the team typically develops t=
he fix on the oldest affected release branch and then cherry-picks the chan=
ges to newer versions. This approach ensures that the fix works correctly o=
n the most constrained version and typically adapts well to newer versions =
with minimal modification.</p>
<p>For bugs that require different approaches across versions due to archit=
ectural changes or other differences, the team may develop separate fixes f=
or each version. This approach requires more development effort but ensures=
 that each fix is optimized for its target version and does not introduce u=
nnecessary complexity or risk.</p>
<p>Testing requirements for multi-release fixes are more extensive than sin=
gle-version fixes because the team must verify the fix across all affected =
versions and their respective deployment environments. Each version receive=
s independent testing to ensure that the fix resolves the issue without int=
roducing regressions. The testing may reveal that the fix requires modifica=
tion for specific versions, leading to version-specific commits within the =
overall fix effort.</p>
<p>Deployment coordination becomes critical for security fixes and other ur=
gent updates that affect multiple production versions. The team typically d=
eploys fixes to older versions first, as these represent the highest risk d=
ue to their longer exposure to the vulnerability. Staggered deployments all=
ow monitoring of the fix impact and provide opportunities to halt the deplo=
yment process if unexpected issues arise.</p>
<p>The classification of bugs affects how they are prioritized and managed =
across multiple releases. Critical bugs, including security vulnerabilities=
, data corruption risks, and system stability issues, receive immediate att=
ention across all affected versions. High priority bugs may be included in =
the next scheduled maintenance release for each affected version. Standard =
bugs might only be fixed in currently active release branches, with older v=
ersions receiving the fix only if specifically requested by customers.</p>
<p>Communication about multi-release fixes requires careful coordination to=
 ensure that all stakeholders understand which versions are affected, when =
fixes will be available, and what actions they may need to take. Release no=
tes for each affected version document the fix and its impact, while securi=
ty advisories provide additional detail for security-related fixes.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-Hotfixes">Hotfixes</h2>
<p>Emergency situations require expedited processes while maintaining the q=
uality and review standards that protect the codebase and production system=
s. The definition of an emergency includes security vulnerabilities that po=
se immediate risk, data corruption or loss scenarios, system outages that a=
ffect customer operations, and compliance violations that could result in r=
egulatory action.</p>
<p>When an emergency is declared, the response begins with rapid assessment=
 to understand the scope of the problem, identify affected systems and vers=
ions, and determine the appropriate response strategy. This assessment driv=
es decisions about which release branches require fixes, what testing can b=
e performed within the available timeframe, and how to coordinate communica=
tions with stakeholders.</p>
<p>Hotfix branch creation follows the same patterns as normal bug fixes but=
 with compressed timelines and elevated priority. The hotfix branch is crea=
ted from the appropriate release branch, and development begins immediately=
 with focus on the minimal change necessary to resolve the emergency. The p=
rinciple of minimal change is crucial during emergencies because it reduces=
 the risk of introducing additional problems while addressing the immediate=
 crisis.</p>
<p>Code review remains mandatory even during emergency situations. While th=
e review process may be expedited through immediate reviewer availability a=
nd focused review scope, the requirement for peer review cannot be bypassed=
. Experience has consistently shown that emergency situations are precisely=
 when human error is most likely, making the extra set of eyes provided by =
code review invaluable for preventing additional problems.</p>
<p>The expedited review process may involve verbal pre-review where the dev=
eloper explains the changes to a reviewer before submitting the pull reques=
t, immediate reviewer assignment to ensure prompt attention, and focused re=
view scope that concentrates on the correctness of the emergency fix rather=
 than broader code quality considerations. Despite these accommodations, th=
e review must still verify that the fix addresses the emergency without int=
roducing new risks.</p>
<p>Testing during emergencies balances speed with safety by focusing on the=
 most critical validation activities. This testing includes verification th=
at the fix resolves the emergency situation, confirmation that the fix does=
 not break core system functionality, and validation that the fix can be sa=
fely deployed to production environments. Automated testing provides rapid =
feedback, while manual testing focuses on the specific emergency scenario a=
nd related functionality.</p>
<p>Emergency deployment follows expedited processes that may bypass normal =
change management procedures while maintaining essential safety checks. The=
 deployment process includes verification of the deployment target environm=
ent, confirmation that rollback procedures are available and tested, and mo=
nitoring setup to detect any issues introduced by the emergency fix.</p>
<p>Communication during emergencies requires regular updates to stakeholder=
s about the response progress, estimated resolution timeline, and any actio=
ns they may need to take. The communication plan includes immediate notific=
ation of key stakeholders, regular status updates throughout the response p=
rocess, and post-resolution communication about the final outcome and any f=
ollow-up actions required.</p>
<p>Post-emergency activities include conducting a thorough post-mortem to u=
nderstand how the emergency occurred, what response processes worked well, =
and what improvements could prevent similar situations or improve response =
effectiveness. The post-mortem results in specific action items that addres=
s both the underlying cause of the emergency and any process improvements i=
dentified during the response.</p>
<p>The application of emergency fixes to multiple release branches follows =
the same multi-release bug management processes but with compressed timelin=
es and coordinated deployment schedules. All affected versions receive the =
emergency fix, but deployment may be staggered to allow monitoring of the f=
ix impact across different environments and customer configurations.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI+Branching&amp;ReleaseStrategy-Automation">Automation</h1>
<p>The AI+ project is currently developing its automation and tooling infra=
structure to support the branching and release processes described in this =
guide. Multiple automation platforms and tools are being evaluated and inte=
grated to create a comprehensive development and deployment pipeline.</p>
<p><strong>Development Status: Work in Progress</strong></p>
<p>The following areas are currently under active development:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Build System Integration</strong>: Evaluating TeamCity for prima=
ry build orchestration and artifact management =E2=80=A2 <strong>Source Con=
trol Automation</strong>: Implementing GitHub Actions for pull request vali=
dation and branch management</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Release Coordination</strong>: Developing scripts and tooling fo=
r multi-repository release branch creation and version management =E2=80=A2=
 <strong>Deployment Pipeline</strong>: Designing automated deployment proce=
sses for staging and production environments =E2=80=A2 <strong>Quality Gate=
s</strong>: Establishing automated testing, security scanning, and code qua=
lity checks =E2=80=A2 <strong>Notification Systems</strong>: Integrating Sl=
ack, email, and JIRA notifications for development lifecycle events =E2=80=
=A2 <strong>Monitoring and Alerting</strong>: Implementing branch health mo=
nitoring and build failure detection =E2=80=A2 <strong>Repository Managemen=
t</strong>: Automating branch protection rules, cleanup processes, and main=
tenance activities</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The automation strategy will focus on reducing manual effort, improving =
consistency, and minimizing human error while maintaining the quality and r=
eview standards outlined in this guide. As these tools and processes mature=
, this section will be updated with comprehensive guidance on their usage a=
nd integration with the overall development workflow.</p>
<p>Teams should continue following the manual processes described throughou=
t this guide while the automation infrastructure is being developed and tes=
ted. The fundamental branching and release strategies remain unchanged rega=
rdless of automation status, and the manual processes provide the foundatio=
n upon which automation will be built.</p>
    </div>
